workedExamples.question_text:
Possible water-pipe routes between six towns are shown. Edge weights are distances in kilometres. Determine the minimum total length of pipe needed to connect all six towns.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.6); \coordinate (B) at (1.6,2.4); \coordinate (C) at (2.1,0); \coordinate (D) at (4.1,3.3); \coordinate (E) at (5.1,0.5); \coordinate (F) at (6.6,2);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above] {$5$};
\draw (A)--(C) node[midway,below left] {$9$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$11$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,above] {$7$};
\draw (C)--(E) node[midway,below] {$10$};
\draw (D)--(E) node[midway,left] {$8$};
\draw (D)--(F) node[midway,above right] {$10$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,below right] {$6$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/left,B/above,C/below,D/above,E/below,F/right}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt); \path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Construct a minimum spanning tree by selecting the lowest-weight edges without forming a cycle: $AB$, $EF$, $BD$, $DE$ and $AC$. All six towns are connected by five edges.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.6); \coordinate (B) at (1.6,2.4); \coordinate (C) at (2.1,0); \coordinate (D) at (4.1,3.3); \coordinate (E) at (5.1,0.5); \coordinate (F) at (6.6,2);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above] {$5$};
\draw (A)--(C) node[midway,below left] {$9$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,above] {$7$};
\draw (D)--(E) node[midway,left] {$8$};
\draw (E)--(F) node[midway,below right] {$6$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/left,B/above,C/below,D/above,E/below,F/right}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt); \path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Sum the selected edge weights.
$\begin{aligned}\text{Minimum length} &= 5+6+7+8+9\\&=35\text{ km}\end{aligned}$
The minimum total length of pipe is $35\text{ km}$.

Each pair of towns gives one undirected edge. Select $BD=2.2$ and $AD=3.1$. Skip $AB=4.2$, which would form a cycle. Select $CD=4.6$ to connect the remaining town.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2); \coordinate (B) at (4,2); \coordinate (C) at (4,0); \coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\draw (A)--(D) node[midway,left] {$3.1$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,above,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$2.2$};
\draw (C)--(D) node[midway,below] {$4.6$};
\foreach \v/\p in {A/above,B/above,C/right,D/left}{\fill (\v) circle (2pt); \path (\v) node[\p] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}\text{Minimum length} &=2.2+3.1+4.6\\&=9.9\text{ km}\end{aligned}$
Multiply the total length by the cost per kilometre.
$\begin{aligned}\text{Minimum cost} &=9.9\times60\,000\\&=\$594\,000\end{aligned}$

